WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.443
The OT threat landscape is the collection of actors, events, weaknesses, access paths,

00:00:05.443 --> 00:00:10.374
and operational conditions that can cause harm to industrial and cyber-physical systems.

00:00:10.374 --> 00:00:14.484
It includes deliberate attacks, ordinary malware, insider actions, vendor risk,

00:00:14.484 --> 00:00:16.128
configuration mistakes, and accidents.

00:00:16.128 --> 00:00:21.059
The landscape is broader than a list of threat groups because operational

00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:25.580
design and human behavior shape what a threat can actually affect.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.456
Operational technology monitors or controls physical processes.

00:00:28.456 --> 00:00:32.977
A cyber event may therefore influence production, equipment, safety, environmental conditions,

00:00:32.977 --> 00:00:34.621
reliability, or public services.

00:00:34.621 --> 00:00:37.909
Loss of a file is a data impact.

00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:40.785
Loss of trustworthy pressure readings, control communications,

00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:43.662
or operator visibility is an operational impact.

00:00:43.662 --> 00:00:45.306
Some incidents create both.

00:00:45.306 --> 00:00:49.005
Threat analysis should connect technical events to process consequences.

00:00:49.005 --> 00:00:53.936
Malware on a business workstation may be contained as an IT event.

00:00:53.936 --> 00:01:00.512
The same malware affecting an engineering workstation may delay maintenance or undermine confidence in controller configurations.

00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:04.621
A network outage may stop reporting while local control continues,

00:01:04.621 --> 00:01:08.320
or it may remove functions required for safe operation.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.964
Context determines the consequence.

00:01:09.964 --> 00:01:14.073
OT risk also depends on independent safeguards and recovery capability.

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:17.772
Mechanical protection, safety systems, local operating procedures, segmentation, backups,

00:01:17.772 --> 00:01:20.238
and trained personnel may limit harm.

00:01:20.238 --> 00:01:23.525
Analysts need to know which protections are independent,

00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:26.813
whether they remain available during a cyber incident,

00:01:26.813 --> 00:01:29.690
and how the organization verifies trusted operation.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.389
Understanding the threat landscape is an ongoing operational practice.

00:01:33.389 --> 00:01:36.676
Maintain visibility into assets, architecture, remote access, vulnerabilities,

00:01:36.676 --> 00:01:39.964
adversary activity, incidents, and changes to the process.

00:01:39.964 --> 00:01:43.252
The objective is not to predict every attack.

00:01:43.252 --> 00:01:47.361
It is to recognize credible pathways, prepare for their consequences,

00:01:47.361 --> 00:01:51.060
and reduce risk without disrupting the operation being protected.
